## Import

Parties can be imported by their full party ID. The party ID is treated as an
opaque string — the provider makes no assumption about its internal structure
(hint, fingerprint, version segments, etc.), so any shape returned by the
participant is accepted (Canton 0.6.2's longer, multi-segment IDs included):

```shell
terraform import canton_party.validator "validator::12205d50b0e00850fb42bb3750f35cf4f9ffdb97827369d0bd1645386bd61bfb23da"
```

After import, `party_id_hint` is set to the segment before the first `::`
(the classical Canton hint position), or to the full party ID when there is
no usable prefix (no `::`, or a leading `::`). Canton may normalize the hint
during allocation, so the extracted prefix may not match the original config
hint exactly — to avoid a `terraform plan` diff, **update `party_id_hint` in
your config to match the imported value**.

Be aware that `party_id_hint` is a force-replacement attribute: changing it
in HCL after import (or at any other time) will cause Terraform to destroy
this resource and allocate a brand-new party. The old party remains on the
ledger — Canton parties cannot be deleted — but Terraform will no longer
track it. Always reconcile the config to the imported hint, never the
other way around.
